//BUG="a template function that calls itself never finds its own instance in the cache"
//OUT="3"

// The instance cache that makes recursion possible
// (`check_instance_cache` in src/compiler/normalizer/mod.rs) compares the
// template types of a call as well as its argument types. When a call does
// not spell its template arguments out, `normalize_function` fills them in
// with brand-new type labels — and consults the cache straight away, before
// those labels have been tied to anything. A new label is equal to nothing,
// so the comparison with the instance that is being built fails, and the
// recursive call starts another instance, whose body makes the same call.
// The compile ends with
//
//     This function is in the 100-th recursive call in the normalization phase.
//
// The return hint rules out the documented cause of that message
// (05_functions/40 and 41). Two things make the function below compile, and
// both show the recursion itself is fine:
//
//   * dropping `[T]` and the hint on `x`, which leaves an ordinary generic
//     function with no template list;
//   * writing the recursive call as `count[T](x n - 1)`, because an explicit
//     template argument is unified with the parameter before the lookup.
//
// Neither is available to a method of a templated struct: the struct's
// parameters are appended to the method's template list by the lowerer and
// cannot be written at the call (see 69).
//
// The fix is the one 69 needs: deduce the template types from the arguments
// before looking the instance up.

fn count[T] x: T n: i32 -> i32
    if n == 0
        ret 0
    ret 1 + count(x n - 1)

fn main
    out "{count(5 3)}"
